  "rationale": "Rich-medium (IMDM) sub-series of the HAP1 genetic-interaction study. The deposited matrix has one raw-count column per sequencing library and no gene column; prepare.R adds Gene (REAGENT_ID prefix), writes blank cells (no reads; the matrix holds no zeros) as 0, and keeps the screens used, summing each screen's endpoint libraries (A/B/C, parallel passages of one infection) into one column <GIN>_end so that the unit of replication is the screen (infection) rather than parallel passages. Library-to-screen mapping (T0 and endpoints A/B/C per GIN screen) comes from the paper's Data S1 and agrees with the GSM titles. Query: PTAR1 14-bp deletion, the paper's showcase query, screened four times in rich medium (GIN003, GIN044, GIN288, GIN298; the four used for the PTAR1 MCMC consensus). Reference: all 16 QC-passing parental WT rich-medium screens (diploid WT clone GIN036 excluded). The T0s of GIN001 (WT) and GIN044 (PTAR1) were shared with minimal-medium screens of the same infection and deposited only in GSE298082, so prepare.R takes those two columns from that matrix. Model ~ screen + phase + gi: screen absorbs each infection's T0 representation, phase (T0, end) is the common dropout, and gi marks the PTAR1 endpoints, so giPTAR1_end is the PTAR1-specific change in guide abundance beyond the WT dropout (a differential log fold change, the quantity qGI scores; negative = negative genetic interaction). With one T0 and one summed endpoint per screen, the residual dispersion is the between-screen variation of the dropout across the WT screens, and the PTAR1 effect is estimated across four independent PTAR1 infections. Different screens ran to different endpoints (T11-T20 doublings); the qGI method adjusts for this with an M-A loess normalization that BARCS does not apply. Controls: 142 EGFP/LacZ/luciferase guides.",
  "counts_md5": "fac01760c6666ba02e3daa00eb05d10b"
}